      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Find the product $EF$, if\n$E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$F=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & -8 & \\sqrt{5} & 14 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{7}{5} & -\\frac{\\pi }{2} & 9 \\\\ \\sqrt{17} & -6 & 0 & \\frac{e}{3} \\\\ -11 & \\sqrt{19} & -4 & -\\operatorname{ln}(3)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $EF=$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that $E$ is the $1$st *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{4}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $4\\times 4$ matrix $F$ by the $1$st *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{4}$ on the *left*, the result must be the $1$st *row* of $F.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nEF=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & -8 & \\sqrt{5} & 14\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}EF & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & -8 & \\sqrt{5} & 14 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{7}{5} & -\\frac{\\pi }{2} & 9 \\\\ \\sqrt{17} & -6 & 0 & \\frac{e}{3} \\\\ -11 & \\sqrt{19} & -4 & -\\operatorname{ln}(3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & -8 & \\sqrt{5} & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
